<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Probus (emperor)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">232–282</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Marcus Aurelius Probus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> Probus was Roman emperor from 276 to 282, a soldier from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. He drove Germanic invaders out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontiers and settled large groups of barbarians, among them Bastarnae, inside the empire. He was killed by his own troops in 282.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/probus-emperor.jpg" alt="Probus (emperor)" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Probus_Musei_Capitolini_MC493.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Probus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> in Pannonia Inferior, the son of Dalmatius. The Historia Augusta gives his father's name as Maximus and says his personal wealth was modest. He entered the army around the age of 25 and rose rapidly. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> appointed him a military tribune at a very young age, and he justified the choice with a victory over the Sarmatians on the Illyrian frontier.</p><p>He became one of Aurelian's highest-placed lieutenants and reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> in 273. In 275 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> made him supreme chief of the east with extraordinary powers, and after Tacitus died in 276 the troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> elected Probus emperor in his camp.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Florian, the half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, had also proclaimed himself emperor, but he was killed by his own soldiers after an indecisive campaign against Probus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>. Probus referred his claim to the Senate in a respectful dispatch, and it ratified it. In 277 he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In 278 he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> against the Alemanni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugii">Lugii</a>, while his generals cleared the province of Frankish and Burgundian raiders. He then crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, repaired the fortifications of Hadrian between the Rhine and Danube, and accepted the submission of nine major tribes, which pledged recruits for his army.</p><p>By demanding a tribute of manpower he set a precedent for bringing foreign soldiers into the empire as auxiliaries on a large scale. In peacetime he set soldiers to useful works, such as planting vineyards in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. In 280 and 281 he put down three usurpers, Julius Saturninus, Proculus and Bonosus, and in the winter of 281 he held a triumph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. He left Rome in 282 to prepare a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Accounts of his death differ. According to Joannes Zonaras, the commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guard</a>, Marcus Aurelius Carus, was proclaimed emperor by his troops; troops Probus sent against him changed sides, and his remaining soldiers killed him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> in September or October 282. Other sources say soldiers who objected to civic work such as draining marshes rebelled against him. Carus became emperor and had the killers put to death.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a>, drawing largely on the Historia Augusta, treated Probus as the last of the benevolent constitutional emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His successor Carus did not seek the Senate's confirmation of his title, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> later undermined its authority. Probus's victories continued the line of soldier emperors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius Gothicus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Probus (emperor)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Frankish pirates ravaged the coasts of Gaul and Britain; Carausius was given command of the Channel fleet against Frankish and Saxon raiders around 285, and a famous story tells of Frankish prisoners settled by <b>Probus</b> on the Black Sea who seized ships and sailed home through the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 280 the emperor <b>Probus</b> settled a large number of Bastarnae in Thrace, after which they disappear as a distinct people.</blockquote><a href="/state/bastarnae">Bastarnae</a></li><li><blockquote>Aurelian responded in 272, defeating the Palmyrene armies in Syria while Roman forces, led according to later sources by the future emperor <b>Probus</b>, recovered Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/palmyrene-egypt">Palmyrene Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.97,19.61&amp;year=264" aria-label="Sirmium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5017 576 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Probus (emperor)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5467 846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5467 846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.97,19.61&amp;year=264">What was here in 264 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
